        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>To determine the effects of microgravity on developmentally programmed expression of Troponin T and I isoforms known to regulate cardiac and skeletal muscle contraction.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>This experiment was performed twice, once during Mir 18 (returning on STS-71) and once during Mir 19 (returning on STS-74). Fertilized eggs were carried to the Mir space station aboard a Russian Progress (227) supply vessel, and placed in an incubator supplied by the Institute for Biomedical Problems. On days 7, 10, 14 and 16, eight eggs were fixed and stored for return to Earth.<P><B>Results:</B><P>During both runs of the experiment, internal incubator temperature was higher than nominal throughout the experiment, and most of the quail embryos failed to develop as expected, resulting in low science return. During Mir 19, ten embryos developed past 7 days and four embryos developed  to an appropriate age. Analysis of the effects of microgravity on isoform expression are still underway.
        </div>

